            <div xml:id="d685">

                <pb facs="0779" n="681" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg681"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1417</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Chargé</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lindsay</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-12"><hi rendition="#italic">March 12,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 158</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my note No. 121 of
                    February 24th, I have the honour to inform you that His Majesty’s Government
                    intend to <pb facs="0780" n="682" xml:id="pg682"/> retain the services of
                    General Beckett on the Technical Board so long as Mr. Stevens remains at Harbin.
                    His Majesty’s Government are therefore anxious to ascertain whether Mr. Stevens
                    is likely to remain, and if so for how long.</p>
                <p>I should accordingly be grateful if you could afford me some information on this
                    point to enable me to reply to the enquiry made by the Foreign Office in
                    London.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">R. C. Lindsay</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
